hi there Steve here's the Coffman back  in Palm Springs today I want to talk  about what makes a polyglot and you know  this term polyglot by the way if you  enjoy these videos about language  learning please subscribe  you know this term polyglot which we  hear a lot within the sort of community  of people who learn languages who get  together at polyglot conferences and so  forth  it's a term that a lot of English  speakers don't even use or many of them  don't even know and when I started into  this sort of the last 10 12 years of  active language learning I called myself  a linguist because in Inked people a  linguist is someone who is good at  languages in fact that's the definition  in the dictionary and the word polygon  has has grown in use recently its more  common in other languages but  nevertheless what makes a polyglot what  makes a linguist  and I think a big factor is luck okay  circumstance for example I arrived in  Palm Springs at the airport we I go to  over to look for my uber driver and  message comes back that the driver is on  his way his name is Abdul Wow  Abdul maybe maybe he speaks Arabic so  when I meet Abdul I say assalam alaikum  and of course he is an Arab speaker he's  from Sudan he's very happy we talked  have a lively discussion in the car that  kind of thing is motivating kind of  recharges my batteries not that I can  say very much to him if anything but  that kind of gets you going so other  examples of luck for example I was born  in Sweden so for the first five years of  my life I spoke Swedish and then I moved  to Canada and all of a sudden spoke  English and no longer spoke Swedish and  I can't even remember when that  transition took place  we spoke English at home but I've always  felt that that might have contributed to  me at least being interested in  languages and according to some theories  it makes them  more flexible and it makes it easier to  learn other languages so when I was at  the language long fest festival dalaran  language festival in Montreal and I was  giving a talk to a roomful of say 300  interested polyglots linguists language  learners I asked how many people in the  room were born in a monolingual family  like English or French speaking in the  case of Montreal just about everybody  raised their head so maybe being born in  Sweden to parents who were actually  German speaking mostly he spoke some  checked maybe all of that helped me but  all these people in the room whom I met  there he spoke eight ten twelve  languages they did not have that benefit  so something else triggered very  interested in languages interest in  languages but something there has to be  effectively as I've said before when I  was 17 years old I just spoke English I  had French at school I wasn't very  interested and then I had a professor at  McGill who turned me on to French  civilization French culture French  literature French history and  subsequently I went to France so that  was a luck something I could have had a  professor in Spanish or a professor in  you know law or something that would  have made me interested in that subject  so that was a circumstance that happened  you know and as a result of that then  again a friend told me that they were  there was a diplomatic you know an exam  to get into the Canadian Foreign Service  at the Embassy in Paris I wrote it I was  accepted that was luck again then the  Canadian Foreign Service decided they  needed someone to learn Chinese so I  started learning Chinese on my own and  then told them about it then they sent  me off to Hong Kong to learn Chinese  then I had a disagreement with the  person who was going to go to Beijing  with me and I said I'll go to Japan I  learned Japanese on my own but I don't  want to be in an isolated post like  Beijing with this person with it with  whom I have serious disagreement and  they said yes so then that was another  circumstance  made me learn japanese and i could go on  and on but the point is that there has  to be something that triggers that  interest because motivation is such a  big part of language learning and very  often that motivation is a matter of  chance there's a reason why some people  get interested in learning languages  other people get interested in designing  aircraft or becoming landscape gardeners  something triggers it and I should say  one more thing just to finish on this so  luck  I think circumstance luck is a trigger  and it's very important obviously you  also have to then put in the time and  and I think this is similar to a lot of  things in life I think people  underestimate the degree to which luck  determines it so much in life I can say  that in terms of my own business career  I know that it was more than anything  else  luck that determined whatever success I  had in business someone that I met  things that worked out that maybe  shouldn't have worked out but did work  out things that I couldn't have  anticipated which happened to me and I  think we have to always remember that  and that is why I think it is very  important in a society that we organize  our society in such a way that we  redistribute the opportunity that we  make it possible for some of those who  may have had less fortune that they or  maybe the next generation has access to  the same educational opportunities the  same health opportunities and and  therefore you know the fact that in many  countries of the world inequality is  increasing is not a good thing and it's  not a good thing for those societies and  it's not fair given the degree to which  income inequality is mostly a matter of  luck not a matter of some people work  hard now there's known it's largely a  matter of luck and so I just wanna I  just was thinking about you know luck is  a  factor yeah we can do things to increase  our opportunities we can do things to  increase the chance that luck will come  our way we have to take advantage of  things to come our way but the fact  remains that luck is a big factor and  that should force us to be a little  humble that's my message for today  thank you for listening bye for now
 

